p('We examined whether the global P. aeruginosa collection contains an open pangenome with a conserved backbone and a continually expanding accessory repertoire, and compared conserved gene content and gene frequencies between clinical and environmental genomes. Within the clinical collection, we characterized predicted functional repertoires, cross-functional associations, gene-content heterogeneity and core-genome lineage diversity. Exploratory accessory-genome clustering was used to summarize structure; it was not treated as evidence of validated epidemiological subpopulations.',[27,28])
p('These analyses provide a framework for subsequent phylogeny-aware antimicrobial-resistance investigation and assessment of conserved diagnostic or vaccine candidates. Resistance prediction and reverse-vaccinology screening remain future translational directions; no completed resistance-prediction model or vaccine-candidate screening result is reported here.',[27,28,178])
