# Abstract is finalized only after Results/Methods/Discussion and display assembly.
items[abstract_index]['text']='Pseudomonas aeruginosa has a plastic genome that complicates population surveillance and interpretation of antimicrobial-resistance determinants. We characterized pangenome architecture in a curated collection of 1,595 genomes, comprising 1,350 clinical, 99 environmental and 146 unknown or other-source genomes, assembled from an initial 4,454 public records. The complete pangenome contained 43,001 clusters: 4,551 core, 673 soft-core, 1,183 shell and 36,594 cloud clusters. Singletons and doubletons numbered 12,293 and 4,400, respectively. The clinical pangenome contained 40,811 clusters, including 34,315 cloud clusters. Whole-dataset accumulation followed P(n)=5060.65n^0.2903 (R²=0.99998); the clinical fit across 5,000 genome-order permutations yielded γ=0.288518 (R²=0.999955), supporting an open repertoire within the sampled collections. A shared-framework comparison identified 4,174 clusters at core-level frequency in both clinical and environmental genomes. Functional profiling of clinical genomes resolved 1,051 predicted AMP homolog families, 204 normalized AMR families, 6,690 BGC domain-signature families and 73 CAZyme families. Most AMP predictions had single-predictor support, and computational calls were not interpreted as experimental phenotypes. Clinical genomic fluidity was 0.10736 (95% jackknife confidence interval, 0.10531–0.10941). Core-SNP phylogeny recovered compact clusters and divergent lineages, including a strongly supported 25-isolate clade. These findings describe a conserved backbone within an extensive accessory repertoire, with heterogeneous predicted functions and gene content. Unequal source sampling, incomplete metadata and population structure constrain source and association interpretations, motivating phylogeny-aware surveillance and targeted functional validation.'
